/*
 * EmitsNested.ag — types declared inside types.
 *
 * Copyright © 2026 Kyle J. Cardoza. MIT licence (see docs/LICENSE).
 *
 * `Bar` declared inside `Foo` is the type `Foo.Bar` in Sterling, and Objective-C
 * has no nesting, so it is emitted as a class of its own under a MANGLED name:
 * the dot becomes an underscore, `Foo.Bar` -> `Foo_Bar`.
 *
 * The mangling is deliberately LEXICAL — the dot is the whole rule — which keeps
 * `map_type` a pure name-to-name function with no symbol table behind it, so a
 * reference resolves the same way whether the type is in this file, another one,
 * or imported. What that costs is stated in the plan: a top-level class literally
 * named `Outer_Inner` would collide with `Inner` nested in `Outer`.
 *
 * Three facts this file pins down, none of which a non-nested specimen can:
 *
 *   - `Outer.Inner` is a TYPE, so a property of it is a REFERENCE:
 *     `Outer_Inner *item`, not `Outer_Inner item`. §4's table has never heard of
 *     a locally declared class, so the pointer is added from what the file
 *     declares.
 *   - the nested class is emitted BEFORE the class it is nested in, so a property
 *     of it is a complete type rather than a forward-declared pointer;
 *   - and `@class` lines appear for both, because §3 makes the emitter manage
 *     them — which is what lets `Standalone` name `Outer` and `Outer` name its
 *     own nested type regardless of the order they are written in.
 */
class Outer: Object {
    class Inner: Object {
        property depth: Int32
    }

    property item: Outer.Inner
    property label: String

    method make() -> Outer.Inner {
        return self.item;
    }
}

class Standalone: Object {
    property outer: Outer
    property note: String
}
